Hi [~accountid:6151cf6878e5e40070fb74c2],

Although I have already shared with you the external counsel’s advice on this matter via email, I am copying it below for our records. 

  (external counsel’s answer in blue)

# *Products return procedures*

 Our business team would like to be able to return the products they receive from suppliers in the following ways: 

* The supplier delivers the goods. 
* Bolt does not check the merchandise at the time of delivery but accepts it by signing the delivery note. 
* Bolt identifies defective goods within 24 hours of goods delivery. 
* Bolt sends a notification of defective goods to the supplier. 
* The supplier issues a credit note for defective goods. 
* Bolt returns the defective merchandise to the supplier. 

  

Considering the above described procedure, could you please: 

# confirm that there are no obstacles to using this process for returns under local law 

{color:#0747a6}There are no obstacles under Portuguese law to use this process. In commercial purchase and sale agreements, the buyer has up to eight days to inspect the goods delivered and potentially raise a claim. {color}  

2. confirm whether, in accordance with Portuguese law, the documents that must be issued to support the return procedure are the following: 

A.    delivery note signed by Bolt and the supplier upon delivery of the goods; 

B.    email to the supplier regarding deficient goods; and  

C.   credit note issued by the supplier for deficient goods. 

{color:#0747a6}We confirm that these documents must be issued to support the return procedure. In particular, documents A. and C. are issued by the supplier. Document B. is issued by Bolt. {color}

{color:#0747a6}We note that a {color}{color:#0747a6}+step D. is required+{color}{color:#0747a6}, assuming that it is Bolt who will proceed with the devolution of the defective merchandise (i.e., will transport the defective goods, instead of having the supplier come to collect them): {color}

* {color:#0747a6}In order for Bolt to return the defective products to the supplier, Bolt must issue a {color}{color:#0747a6}+devolution note+{color}{color:#0747a6} (“nota de devolução”), which will work as the transport document (“documento de transporte”) that will accompany the defective merchandise, in order to comply with the circulation of goods regime. {color}

{color:#0747a6}  {color}

* {color:#0747a6}This transport document must be communicated to the Tax Authority before the transport takes place. If the devolution note is issued via Bolt’s accounting and billing software, the communication to the Tax Authority is done by electronic transmission of data (like it happens with the issuance of an invoice). {color}

{color:#0747a6}  {color}

* {color:#0747a6}The transport document must be processed in three copies: the original and the duplicate must accompany the goods (the first one is for the supplier and the second one is for the inspection authorities), and the triplicate will be kept by Bolt for its records. {color}

  

3. let us know if it is mandatory to have any other documentation signed by the supplier as proof of return of the goods according to Portuguese law. 

{color:#0747a6}No other documentation needs to be signed by the supplier as proof of return of the goods according to Portuguese law. {color}

  

*2. Stock acceptance*

Due to the rapid nature of deliveries, Bolt is unable to check all products at the time of delivery. Therefore, our sales team wishes to accept all deliveries received from suppliers and notify suppliers of defective products within 24 hours of receiving the products. 

Could you confirm whether: 

# There are any legal limitations / obstacles to this approach based on Portuguese Law. 

{color:#0747a6}We do not find any legal limitations / obstacles to this approach. {color}



2. There is a mandatory obligation to check all products at the time of delivery. If so,  

{color:#0747a6}There is no mandatory obligation to check all products at the time of delivery. In case the supplier asks Bolt to check the products at the time of delivery, Bolt can just state such inspection is impossible to perform at that moment. {color}

 {color:#0747a6}Even though there is no mandatory obligation to check all products at the time of delivery, Bolt can add the following clause in the Supplier Contract for precautionary reasons: {color}

{color:#0747a6}  {color}

{color:#0747a6}_’Bolt has no obligation to check the goods delivered by the supplier upon delivery. Acceptance of goods upon  delivery shall in no way be considered as a waiver of claims regarding the quality or quantity of the goods.  Bolt is entitled to raise claims regarding deficient goods to the supplier within 24 hours after the delivery  of the goods.’_{color}{color:#0747a6} {color}

  

*3. Storage of delivery notes*



# Is Bolt required to keep originals of delivery notes or would a scanned copy be sufficient? 

{color:#0747a6}A scanned copy of the originals is sufficient, as long as the copy is perfectly legible, without loss of resolution or information. {color}

  2. How long must Bolt store delivery notes under Portuguese Law? 

{color:#0747a6}Bolt must store the delivery notes until the end of the 4th year following the date of their issuance. {color}

----

Follow- up question: *Is our understanding correct that if Bolt is not transporting or arranging for the transport of the goods back to the supplier in the event of returns, then Bolt will not be required to submit a return note?*

{color:#0747a6}Your understanding is correct. Bolt does not need to issue a return note. The credit note issued by the supplier for deficient goods will work as the transport document.{color}

