Hello team, Emilie thanks a lot for the feedback:)

few comments on my side, happy to further discuss:

*1.Regulatory risks*

* +The solution would therefore be to use an entity other than Bolt Portugal for the purposes of the co-financing.+ [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f]{color:#ff5630} is it possible? {color}{color:#36b37e}This shouldn’t be an issue to my understanding - Acting guarantor party could be Bolt Operations OU (to be validated){color}
* There is, of course, the risk that if another Bolt entity (other than Bolt Portugal) is used for this purpose, the connection be used to claim that it is Bolt Portugal indirectly. However, considering the formalistic approach enshrined in the law, we consider that there is a strong argument in favour of only considering the formal holder of the license in Portugal to be prevented from financing the acquisition of vehicles. +A possibility that would reduce the risk even further would be to use a partner with no corporate connection to Bolt+. We recall that you have e.g. a partner for (re)invoicing purposes: you might have partners for the co-financing. [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f]{color:#ff5630} is it possible? {color}{color:#36b37e}Could we discuss this option, not very comfortable of adding a non related party to this structure {color}

*b) Financing contract*

* Without prejudice to the above, and in case the Bolt entity that concludes the contract is deemed as the platform operator and thereby caught by the prohibition, it is our understanding that it is possible to argue that the contract in question is not a financing contract (which is prohibited by law as mentioned above), but rather an investment contract, given that there is no return on capital. Further measures to eliminate typical features of a financing contract (such as risk) could be adopted, such as +putting in place a warranty+ that eliminates all risk. {color:#36b37e}Correct, the way i see it is:{color}
** {color:#36b37e}+*Financing agreement*+{color}{color:#36b37e}  to be executed between the Bank and the FO{color}
** {color:#36b37e}the equivalent of a {color}{color:#36b37e}+*limited Third party guarantee*+{color}{color:#36b37e} between the Bank and Bolt to provide xx amount as a limited guarantor of the FO {color}
** {color:#36b37e}+*Marketing agreement*+{color}{color:#36b37e} between Bolt and FO with binding terms on exclusivity, GMV target and adjustment of payment at end of period if target not achieved {color}

·         In the proposed agreement, we understand there is an actual purchase of cars and an actual hiring of such cars to operate under the Bolt platform. In this sense we believe there are good reasons to argue that, in the 5% cash back, one +should identify: (i) a certain type of discount, (ii) a delayed payment by Bolt in case the GMV threshold is exceed, in relation to the difference paid on the excess GMV (which is one of the situations typically considered as exempt from the qualification of the activity of granting of credit on a professional basis) and (iii) a contractual penalty to be paid by the Contractor when the GMV threshold is not reached+, in relation to the amount paid in relation to the GMV shortcoming. [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f]{color:#ff5630} I need to clarify this with DLA{color}



# Monitoring and Security

·         We will include in the draft agreement monitoring rights as requested.

·         In relation to security, please note that in relation to cars the common Portuguese practice is to not include any security, except for a +retention of title (++_reserva de propriedade_++). We believe this solution could be explored with the Contractor+, but it should be noted that when a retention of title is granted in favour of an entity that is not the seller of the asset, it may be challenged in court. [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f]{color:#ff5630} is it something you want to check with the FO?{color}{color:#36b37e} security over the cars would be provided to the financing bank as part of the transaction which would definitely not want to share any type of security in pari pasu -  I don’t believe it is worth exploring a second rank guarantee, this is a risk that business should accept to sponsor with the mitigant of the marketing agreement that we would have in place{color}

·         Alternatives (or add-ons) to the retention of title that we have seen in the past would be +requesting a bank guarantee or an insurance policy+, although costs would be involved, or a parent company guarantee. [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f]{color:#ff5630} is there a chance the FO would provide that or not? {color}{color:#36b37e}Same as the above {color}

